      "text": "To find the cost of a pencil, we start by defining the variables for the cost of a pen and a pencil. Let \\( p \\) be the cost of a pen in cents and \\( c \\) be the cost of a pencil in cents. We are given two equations based on the problem statement:\n\n1. \\( 4p + 3c = 224 \\) (since $2.24 is equivalent to 224 cents)\n2. \\( 2p + 5c = 154 \\) (since $1.54 is equivalent to 154 cents)\n\nWe will solve this system of linear equations step by step. First, we will eliminate one of the variables. To do this, we can multiply the second equation by 2 to align the coefficients of \\( p \\):\n\n\\[ 2(2p + 5c) = 2(154) \\]\n\\[ 4p + 10c = 308 \\]\n\nNow we have the following system of equations:\n1. \\( 4p + 3c = 224 \\)\n2. \\( 4p + 10c = 308 \\)\n\nNext, we subtract the first equation from the second equation to eliminate \\( p \\):\n\n\\[ (4p + 10c) - (4p + 3c) = 308 - 224 \\]\n\\[ 4p + 10c - 4p - 3c = 84 \\]\n\\[ 7c = 84 \\]\n\\[ c = \\frac{84}{7} \\]\n\\[ c = 12 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pencil is \\( 12 \\) cents. To find the cost of a pen, we substitute \\( c = 12 \\) back into one of the original equations. We will use the first equation:\n\n\\[ 4p + 3(12) = 224 \\]\n\\[ 4p + 36 = 224 \\]\n\\[ 4p = 224 - 36 \\]\n\\[ 4p = 188 \\]\n\\[ p = \\frac{188}{4} \\]\n\\[ p = 47 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pen is \\( 47 \\) cents. To confirm, we substitute \\( p = 47 \\) and \\( c = 12 \\) back into the second original equation:\n\n\\[ 2(47) + 5(12) = 94 + 60 = 154 \\]\n\nThe equations are consistent, so the cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cents.",
