      "text": "Let \\( p \\) represent the cost of one pen in cents, and \\( q \\) represent the cost of one pencil in cents. We are given two equations based on the problem statement:\n\n1. Four pens and three pencils cost \\$2.24. Converting this to cents, we get:\n\\[ 4p + 3q = 224. \\]\n\n2. Two pens and five pencils cost \\$1.54. Converting to cents, we get:\n\\[ 2p + 5q = 154. \\]\n\nWe now have the system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n4p + 3q = 224, \\\\\n2p + 5q = 154.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate one of the variables, we can use the method of elimination. First, we can eliminate \\( p \\) by making the coefficients of \\( p \\) in both equations the same. We do this by multiplying the first equation by 1 and the second equation by 2:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n4p + 3q = 224, \\\\\n4p + 10q = 308.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the first equation from the second equation to eliminate \\( p \\):\n\n\\[\n(4p + 10q) - (4p + 3q) = 308 - 224,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n7q = 84.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( q \\):\n\n\\[\nq = \\frac{84}{7} = 12.\n\\]\n\nSo, the cost of one pencil is \\( 12 \\) cents. To find the cost of one pen, we substitute \\( q = 12 \\) back into the first original equation:\n\n\\[\n4p + 3(12) = 224,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n4p + 36 = 224.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 36 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n4p = 188.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( p \\):\n\n\\[\np = \\frac{188}{4} = 47.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the cost of a pen is 47 cents and the cost of a pencil is 12 cents. The cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).",
