      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let the cost of a pen be \\( p \\) cents and the cost of a pencil be \\( q \\) cents. We are given two equations based on the problem statement:\n\n1. \\( 4p + 3q = 224 \\) (since \\( 2.24 \\) dollars is equivalent to \\( 224 \\) cents)\n2. \\( 2p + 5q = 154 \\) (since \\( 1.54 \\) dollars is equivalent to \\( 154 \\) cents)\n\nWe can solve this system of linear equations. First, we will eliminate one of the variables by making the coefficients of \\( p \\) the same in both equations. We can do this by multiplying the first equation by 2 and the second equation by 4:\n\n\\[ 2(4p + 3q) = 2(224) \\implies 8p + 6q = 448 \\]\n\\[ 4(2p + 5q) = 4(154) \\implies 8p + 20q = 616 \\]\n\nNow we have the system:\n\\[ 8p + 6q = 448 \\]\n\\[ 8p + 20q = 616 \\]\n\nNext, we subtract the first equation from the second equation to eliminate \\( p \\):\n\\[ (8p + 20q) - (8p + 6q) = 616 - 448 \\]\n\\[ 14q = 168 \\]\n\\[ q = \\frac{168}{14} = 12 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pencil is \\( 12 \\) cents. To verify, we can substitute \\( q = 12 \\) back into one of the original equations to find \\( p \\). Using the first equation:\n\\[ 4p + 3(12) = 224 \\]\n\\[ 4p + 36 = 224 \\]\n\\[ 4p = 188 \\]\n\\[ p = \\frac{188}{4} = 47 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pen is \\( 47 \\) cents. To check, we substitute \\( p = 47 \\) and \\( q = 12 \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ 2(47) + 5(12) = 94 + 60 = 154 \\]\n\nThe solution satisfies both equations, confirming that the cost of a pencil is indeed \\( 12 \\) cents. Therefore, the answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{12} \\]",
